A||||9||TRiAD||How Many MP3s...?||||1||
Z||000000||TRiAD||12-19-2001||08:56 PM||||Trying to decide what I'm going to do for music once I inevitably get the Fiero running again, and having found an MP3 source on the net, I've been a downloading fool. <P>I want to fit as much of this into the car as possible, so MP3s are an  obvious choice. <P>I know there are MP3-compatible headunits, but are there MP3-compatible <I>changers</I>?<P>Also, 700MB discs are getting cheaper every day.<P>The questions;<P>1) what MP3 headunits are there out there?<BR>2) are ther MP3 changers?<BR>3) how many MP3s CAN you fit on a 700MB disc?<P>I called up 100 MP3s from my list, and it was 474MB...does this mean I could fit 150-160 MP3s on a single disc?! <P>Thakns all!<BR><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://communities.msn.com/_Secure/0NgAAAMsSN*Wryq60N*CeWB!hXjfeIaMEc1YxPH9aPzaIXm!17ZpdBFE0Yk*d*dHNFG*GR3SFyLa8KJgh1IKe2Q/xmas-sig.jpg">       <BR><B><I><FONT COLOR="red">Merry Christmas!</FONT><BR><FONT COLOR="blue">Project MR1 ~ triadtuning@hotmail.com</B><BR>Photoshop rendition of planned modifications<BR>AIM ~ Michael Blue 72</I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000001||California Kid||12-19-2001||08:59 PM||||Answer to number 3 is approx 150, depends on length of each song.||||reg||1
Z||000002||TRiAD||12-19-2001||11:33 PM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/cool.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_wtf.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_watchout.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_brow.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_dizzy.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_drool.gif"> <P>Holy Crap! That's what I thought! <P>Who needs a CD changer anymore?<P>OhhhhhYeahhhhhh!<BR>||||reg||6
Z||000003||btoth||12-19-2001||11:40 PM||||Number of MP3s depends on the bitrate they are recorded at.  At 128kbps they average 1 minute per MB.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Nashco||12-20-2001||03:30 AM||||Go to ebay and search for "aiwa mp3" and you will come up with their mp3 head unit.  It is a pretty decent head unit and plays mp3 cds, all for about 200 bucks.  I have the cd version of that player, got it from Crutchfield for about 120 bucks!  Two years later it is still going strong and is still hard to skip; has amp outputs and auxiliary input up front as well...I figured it'd be crappy and not last but I've been very impressed!<P>mp3 number on a cd totally depends on quality.  I stick with 128, as it is the best quality for size IMO.  After 128 I can hardly tell the difference.<P>I have yet to see an mp3 changer, but I guarantee they're in the works and will be available affordably soon! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  I think Rockford Fosgate makes an mp3 memory bay, but it's pricy and didn't take cds I don't think.  I don't remember the specifics on that unit, just remember I wasn't interested.<P>Yes, mp3s kick ass, no doubt in my mind! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  Where are you downloading from?  So far Morpheus has been my favorite, since Scour shut down and re-formatted.<P>------------------<BR>Bryce<BR> <IMG SRC="http://home1.gte.net/jbnash/nashcosig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Blacktree||12-20-2001||03:55 AM||||<I><B>1) what MP3 headunits are there out there?</B></I><P>There are about a half dozen sold by Crutchfield. Check out <A HREF="http://www.crutchfield.com" TARGET=_blank>www.crutchfield.com</A>  for details. BTW, that Aiwa head unit mentioned above might be OK for the stock sound system or a low end aftermarket system, but its low signal/noise ratio (only 85dB) is going to become evident in a higher end sound system. To give you a reference, here are the signal/noise ratios of several types of media:<P>audio casettes : about 55-75dB (depending on casette type)<BR>minidisc : about 85-95dB<BR>CD audio : about 93-95dB<BR>DVD audio : about 100dB<P>So if you want the best sound quality from both your MP3 CD's AND your regular audio CD's, look for a head unit with a signal/noise ratio of about 95dB.<P><I><B>2) are ther MP3 changers?</B></I><BR> <BR>I don't think there are any on the market yet.<P><I><B>3) how many MP3s CAN you fit on a 700MB disc?</B></I><P>As mentioned above, it depends on the compression (e.g. bitrate). The higher the bitrate, the better the quality, but larger file size. The lower the bitrate, the smaller the file, with reduced sound quality. As already stated, with a 128KB/s bitrate (which is pretty much a standard), you can fit about 1 minute per MB on your CD. If you encode your own MP3's, don't go below a 128KB/s bitrate. Anything lower will sound like crap. Personaly, I prefer to encode using variable bitrate (averaging about 160KB/s) to get almost-CD-quality sound.<P>[EDIT] Oh, I almost forgot... most of the MP3 head units can only decode 128KB/s MP3 files. If you want one that supports variable-bitrate MP3's, you'll end up having to buy a high-end unit. When checking out the specs of head units, keep an eye out for the supported bitrate(s). [/EDIT]<P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.tweaktech.com" TARGET=_blank>Tweaktech</A><p>[This message has been edited by Blacktree (edited 12-20-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||TRiAD||12-20-2001||09:55 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Nashco:<BR><B>...Where are you downloading from?  So far Morpheus has been my favorite, since Scour shut down and re-formatted.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>NOCONES turned me on to <A HREF="http://www.audiogalaxy.com" TARGET=_blank>www.audiogalaxy.com</A>  . It's not as easy as Napster, since you have no idea how fast the "other" guy is, and you can't go check someone's entire collection if they have something really nice. <P>But, I have been able to get LOTS of stuff there...I'll have to look at morpheus as well...<P>Thanks all!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Fleabitdog||12-20-2001||10:21 AM||||Triad,<BR> I have been using morpheus and i think it rocks!!! Anymore instead of being on the forum 99% of the time I am d/loading stuff. I am addicted. Will have to get a larger hard drive soon or a burner to free  up some space. Dave||||reg||1
Z||000008||Master_Sushi||12-20-2001||01:47 PM||||I can tell a big diff between most 128 and 168+ bitrate mp3s. I normally don't like to download 128 because they seem to have crapy highs and sometimes sounds muffled. Maybe im jsut pickey though  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif">.||||reg||1
Z||000009||onfire||12-20-2001||03:15 PM||||I used to have the Rockford Fosgate mp3 player that Nashco was talking about. I had it in my truck. It used multimedia memory cards. I bought it because I go offroad alot. And I wanted something that wouldn't skip. I have since sold it and bought the Kenwood z828. As the memory cards were to exspensive at the time. <P>Just goto ebay and search under car audio, for mp3. Their are alot of stereo's now that play mp3's. There's even one at Walmart right now for $129.00 <P>150 songs on one cd is not bad. Heck I don't even like 150 songs.I try to download 160k songs. 128k is the bare minimum. IMHO. I currently have about 2gigs of music, mostly for friends that have slow internet connections. I download them then put the songs on a disk for them. <P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/onfiresig.gif"> ||||reg||14
